WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.240
I wanna make sure that
I talk about your movie

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:05.370
which I watched last night.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.297
Which I really enjoyed.

00:00:07.297 --> 00:00:09.630
"Luckiest Girl Alive."

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:12.450
And I know this is an
important project for you,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.550
'cause you're also a producer, right?

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:15.383
Yeah.

00:00:15.383 --> 00:00:16.273
This was a book.

00:00:16.273 --> 00:00:17.106
It was a book.

00:00:17.106 --> 00:00:18.060
Yeah, it was a New York Times bestseller.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.420
It's made up stories and
pictures, starring myself

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.390
and our you know, Orchard Farms
and with Netflix we did it.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:25.470
Well, I'm super proud of it.

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:27.030
It's an amazing fun story.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.490
Well, you tell me what
you think, but be honest.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.780
I will be completely honest with you.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.040
I thought you were fantastic.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.130
And then there's also
an actress who plays you

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:35.963
at a younger age.

00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:36.960
Chiara, yeah.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:37.793
Chiara.

00:00:37.793 --> 00:00:38.626
And it's very tricky,

00:00:38.626 --> 00:00:40.620
because you're, you know,

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.530
you're a very well known iconic person,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:44.580
and when you cut to the,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:46.243
well you are, you mean
you're, you know, come on.

00:00:46.243 --> 00:00:47.670
Thank you!
Mila Kunis.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.650
So, when they cut to a younger Mila Kunis,

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:50.970
it's easy not to buy it.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.590
And I thought she was fantastic.

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.480
I thought there was an amazing kind of

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.270
symbiotic attachment with you two.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.950
Where I completely bought,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.240
when they're cutting back to her,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.110
that this is you at a younger age

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:08.100
and that you have what's
made you as an adult

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.680
is this trauma that you
had when you were younger.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.770
And so I thought that was really well done

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.570
and really well played.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:17.160
It's a tough thing to talk about

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.850
without giving away important.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:19.683
I know.

00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:20.516
I don't wanna give things away,

00:01:20.516 --> 00:01:22.470
because there's a lot of illusion

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.180
to something that
happened to your character

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.590
when you were younger.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.280
And it takes a while for you
to figure out what that is.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:31.500
And I think that's really
important to the story.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.517
So, I think it would
be, I'm not gonna say,

00:01:33.517 --> 00:01:34.830
"Oh and this is what happens,"

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.090
because I think that's something

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.190
that makes the story that
much more compelling.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:40.920
Yeah, it's a really hard movie
to pitch or to talk about.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.780
Because you don't wanna, it's a fun ride.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.340
I mean, it's a heavy topic,

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.650
so I'm not trying to
dilute by saying it's fun,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.410
but it's an exciting
story to watch unfold.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:50.460
I hope, I think.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.320
And you don't wanna take
that away from the viewer.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.700
'Cause there's two things that happen

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:55.440
that are really pinnacle
and really important.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.273
So, when they happen,

00:01:56.273 --> 00:01:57.600
one of them is definitely
a little bit more shocking

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:58.830
than the other I think.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.600
It's something that I think
everybody should experience

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:01.433
on their own,

00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:02.550
versus being told this is about to happen.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:03.383
Being told, yeah.

00:02:03.383 --> 00:02:05.190
The one thing that I think
doesn't give anything away,

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.690
which is a part of your performance

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:11.250
that I thought was very special.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:12.480
Is you're playing someone

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.480
who is trying to repress
something that happened earlier,

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.520
and come across as perfect.

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.200
And so you're very angry,
you're sublimating all of it.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:29.070
And that's something that
comes through really well,

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.200
is you've made yourself as an adult.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.240
This person who's going through the world

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:36.930
who's really strong and
cool and sexy and smart

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.470
and living in Manhattan
and kind of invulnerable

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.200
and then we see that
it takes a huge effort

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.110
for you to be that person,
because you're compensating.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:47.670
Yeah, that's a good way
of explaining it, yeah.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.076
But that's, I mean, as an actor,

00:02:50.076 --> 00:02:52.710
that's a very hard thing to pull off.

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.900
And you do that throughout the film,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.800
which I think is really cool.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.030
It's really fun.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.220
To play somebody who's a little psycho.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.310
Not like super, but just
a little unpredictable,

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.190
and putting on a show is
actually really fun to play.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:06.180
I also had the luxury

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.790
of having the writer on
set with me all the time.

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.040
Who wrote the book and wrote the script,

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.710
who this is slightly
based off of her story.

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.600
And so, I know this sounds odd to say

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.500
for the content of the film;

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.090
but it was one of the most fun productions

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.170
I've ever been on.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.876
And also when you're a
producer on the film as well,

00:03:21.876 --> 00:03:23.130
does that?

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:24.450
I mean every role you do,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.030
it feels like you are completely invested.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:28.440
As an actor,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.823
does anything change when
you're also producing?

00:03:30.823 --> 00:03:31.656
Yes.

00:03:31.656 --> 00:03:33.552
You start realizing what budgets are.

00:03:33.552 --> 00:03:35.430
(everyone laughs)

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.340
I can just see you two going
by the craft service table.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.840
Wait a minute, wait a minute,
wait a minute, wait a minute.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:41.343
That's a lot of casserole.

00:03:42.387 --> 00:03:44.250
And there's a lot of
tuna in the casserole.

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:45.960
There could be less tuna in the casserole,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.230
'cause that stuff isn't cheap.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.063
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:48.063 --> 00:03:48.896
There's that.

00:03:48.896 --> 00:03:49.729
I mean, it is.

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:51.630
You just understand where
your own money comes from.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:53.370
Like, you realize, you're
like, I'm on a double.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:56.310
Like you just realize, why
would I take for myself?

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:57.660
So, there's a difference there.

00:03:57.660 --> 00:03:59.250
You wanna make sure everything happens.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:00.510
Time is of the essence,

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.420
you realize what time
costs in preproduction,

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.490
you understand what setbacks are.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:06.637
That as an actor would be like,

00:04:06.637 --> 00:04:07.860
"Well that's their problem.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:08.880
That is not my problem.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.470
You figure out a new location."

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.922
and now I'm like, ugh, you
gotta go figure this out!

00:04:13.922 --> 00:04:14.970
So, there's that.

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.920
And then you also are much more invested

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:18.690
in the outcome of the film.

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:21.210
So, you know, there's a
little bit more on it.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:22.320
Like, if it's successful

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.580
then I'll be given the right

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.680
to produce more content as a producer.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.850
But if it's, you know what I mean?

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:30.870
There's like, this is a
little bit of new territory.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.520
So, you still have to
build up people's trust.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.730
I had an experience this summer

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.230
where I was asked to be in
a film, not a huge role.

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:42.630
And I'd never done
anything like that before.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.720
And it's not my jam.

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.870
And I never was interested
in being an actor.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.870
But the people making the film
were people I really love.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:54.757
And I was intrigued and I was like,

00:04:54.757 --> 00:04:56.340
"Yeah, okay I'll do this."

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.173
This is a good time.

00:04:57.173 --> 00:04:58.620
I'm not doing the late night show anymore,

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.150
so I gotta try this.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.130
I couldn't believe how hard it is.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:02.963
Oh really?

00:05:02.963 --> 00:05:03.840
It's so different,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:07.443
because what I'm used to, is
get in there, make the show.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.160
They're both hard in their way.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.050
I love 'em, but they're hard in their way.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:17.910
But I couldn't believe how
hard everybody on a film works.

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.092
They're there all day.
all day.

00:05:20.092 --> 00:05:24.300
Everyone's constantly on their feet.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.620
I just, was blown away.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:28.650
I really was impressed.

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:31.710
And afterwards, whenever
I had my last shot,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.420
and they said, "Okay, you
know, we're wrapping Conan."

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:34.253
I just said,

00:05:34.253 --> 00:05:35.330
"I have to tell you people,

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:38.640
I cannot believe how
hard all of you work."

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:42.000
And so now I see films differently.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:43.050
I watch films and I think,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.341
oh my God, to do this
scene with everybody.

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:47.220
Over, and over, and over.
Over, and over, and over.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.990
From every angle and
know that you're there,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:51.060
and know how many people are stepping in,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:52.980
in between takes to make
sure everything's right,

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.390
and relighting.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:55.890
It's an incredible amount of work.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:57.480
I think the only reason I work out,

00:05:57.480 --> 00:05:58.560
or most people work out.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.560
Is to just have the energy
to work for 16 hour days.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.093
Honestly.

00:06:05.093 --> 00:06:06.600
Otherwise you're exhausted.

00:06:06.600 --> 00:06:08.190
And your body takes a beating.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:09.023
From like anybody.

00:06:09.023 --> 00:06:12.150
From grips to any and
all those departments.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.280
They have to be on their
feet all day, every day.

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.170
They're constantly listening
to voices in their heads.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.210
So, it's like insane amount of noise

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:19.950
happening in their brain all the time.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.600
Because of different channels

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.010
and people talking to each other.

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.543
They constantly have to be focusing.

00:06:24.543 --> 00:06:26.370
It is an exhausting job.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.860
Yeah, and again, there's a million vari...

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.830
I mean so many jobs out in
the world are extremely hard.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.467
I just find it very, it always
amuses me when someone says,

00:06:35.467 --> 00:06:39.930
"Man, you know, I'd
like to get into movies.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.840
'Cause I wanna make money
and not have to work."

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.210
And I think, go see what these people do.

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:46.142
Just go see what these people do.

00:06:46.142 --> 00:06:46.975
Yeah, I always say go be a PA.

00:06:46.975 --> 00:06:48.600
Like, if you think you
wanna be in the industry

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:52.590
just be a PA on a film set for one movie.

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.450
And if you enjoy being you being a PA,

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:58.170
'cause PA's are the first
ones in, last ones out.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:06:59.850
They're there for 18 hours.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.670
And they don't get a trailer
to sit in, or a nice office.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:05.010
I mean, it is grueling.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:05.843
Anyways, yeah.

00:07:05.843 --> 00:07:06.676
This was hard for me,

00:07:06.676 --> 00:07:08.223
and I had incredibly lavish things.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.010
I mean, my representation

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.080
made sure that I was massaged every hour.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:13.980
You were pampered!

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:14.850
Oh my god!

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:17.460
Every scene I'm in, I'm in a bubble bath.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:19.500
Which wasn't called for in the script.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:20.430
That was just yours.

00:07:20.430 --> 00:07:21.877
They just roll it in and I'm like,

00:07:21.877 --> 00:07:23.677
"Hello gentlemen!"

00:07:23.677 --> 00:07:25.147
"So, you're the murderer."

00:07:25.147 --> 00:07:25.980
"Yes, I am."

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:26.940
And then they'd roll me out,

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:28.378
and it hurts the movie terribly.

00:07:28.378 --> 00:07:29.790
What is this?

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.869
Oh, the movie is the the guys
from "Saturday Night Live,"

00:07:33.869 --> 00:07:35.670
"Please Don't Destroy."

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:36.503
Yeah.

00:07:36.503 --> 00:07:38.580
And they make these amazing shorts,

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:42.630
and they asked me if I would
do a small thing in their film

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:46.500
and I did and I adore them
and I love their work, and so.

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:47.880
Did you play Conan O' Brien?

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:49.470
No, that was the thing that I...

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:50.430
Oh, you played a character.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:51.263
Yeah, 'cause you know,

00:07:51.263 --> 00:07:53.400
when a talk show host is
in one of those things,

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.882
it's usually,
As a talk show host.

00:07:54.882 --> 00:07:55.715
Yes.

00:07:55.715 --> 00:07:56.910
You go in as a talk show host.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:58.890
And that I got asked
to do a million times,

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.240
and I would do it.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.467
And sometimes I show up
in a movie as, you know,

00:08:02.467 --> 00:08:04.620
"Hi everybody, Conan here
and we're talking to,"

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.590
and you know, "Fake person!"

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:10.770
Who you know, was part of a movie.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.960
And you're part of that reality

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:13.793
but it's not real.

00:08:13.793 --> 00:08:18.360
But this, I got to be someone
who's very different from me,

00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:19.233
which was fun.

00:08:19.233 --> 00:08:20.498
Oh, that's exciting.

00:08:20.498 --> 00:08:21.331
Acting.

00:08:21.331 --> 00:08:22.165
Acting, Conan.

00:08:22.165 --> 00:08:22.998
You're acting.

00:08:22.998 --> 00:08:23.831
Oh, no, no, no.

00:08:23.831 --> 00:08:24.664
I'm, yeah.

00:08:24.664 --> 00:08:25.530
And I'm sure I'll be cut out.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:26.363
It doesn't matter.

00:08:26.363 --> 00:08:27.750
I had a really good experience

00:08:27.750 --> 00:08:29.190
and as long as they send me the footage

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:30.023
I'm cut out of,

00:08:30.023 --> 00:08:31.533
I'll be very happy about that.

